comctl32: Themed buttons paint through a paint buffer, as on Windows.

Lightroom Classic subclasses group boxes, and their WM_ERASEBKGND handler calls ReleaseDC(groupbox, hdc) on the DC it
is given. A themed radio button inside the group box draws its background with DrawThemeParentBackground, which sends
that WM_ERASEBKGND. Wine passed the radio's own BeginPaint DC, the release disabled it, and the radio buttons of "Edit
Photo with Adobe Photoshop" (Photo > Edit In, Ctrl+E) never showed, leaving only their grey descriptions. On Windows 11
the parent gets a memory DC owned by no window (the button paints into a paint buffer), ReleaseDC on it returns 0, and
the radios draw; Windows does release another window's paint DC, so ReleaseDC itself is not the difference. Themed
buttons now paint between BeginBufferedPaint and EndBufferedPaint in WM_PAINT. Repro: wine/tests/paint/radiodc.c
(Windows 11: 0 fails; Wine before: 1).

--- a/dlls/comctl32_v6/button.c
+++ b/dlls/comctl32_v6/button.c
@@ -562,7 +562,22 @@
             int drawState = get_draw_state(infoPtr);
             UINT dtflags = BUTTON_BStoDT(style, GetWindowLongW(hWnd, GWL_EXSTYLE));
 
+            HPAINTBUFFER buffer = NULL;
+            HDC paint_dc = hdc;
+            RECT client;
+
+            /* Windows paints a themed button into a paint buffer: the parent's WM_ERASEBKGND, sent by
+             * DrawThemeParentBackground, gets a memory DC, not the button's paint DC. Lightroom Classic's group
+             * boxes call ReleaseDC on the DC they get there, which must not take the paint DC away. */
+            GetClientRect(hWnd, &client);
+            if (!wParam && (buffer = BeginBufferedPaint(hdc, &client, BPBF_COMPATIBLEBITMAP, NULL, &paint_dc)))
+                hdc = paint_dc;
             btnThemedPaintFunc[btn_type](theme, infoPtr, hdc, drawState, dtflags, infoPtr->state & BST_FOCUS);
+            if (buffer)
+            {
+                EndBufferedPaint(buffer, TRUE);
+                hdc = ps.hdc;
+            }
         }
         else if (btnPaintFunc[btn_type])
         {
